Revision history for App-Syslogd

0.02	Thu Oct  1 2026
	First CPAN release, as App::Syslogd (the backend of
	https://github.com/nigelhorne/syslogd).
	- Logic moved out of etc/syslogd into App::Syslogd, which
	  etc/syslogd now wraps
	- SIGHUP closes and reopens the log file, for logrotate;
	  SIGTERM/SIGINT stop cleanly
	- Security: the log file is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and mode 0600;
	  symlinks, hard links and files owned by other users are refused
	- Proper CSV via Text::CSV; control characters (including
	  newlines) written as \xNN so each record is one line
	- A message without a valid PRI (0-191) is recorded as user.notice,
	  as RFC 3164 4.3.3 requires, rather than as a hex dump
	- Host names via getnameinfo (honours /etc/hosts), cached with CHI;
	  --no-resolve to log addresses; IPv6 and --address supported
	- Datagrams up to 65535 bytes accepted without truncation
	- Messages localisable through Locale::Maketext (English shipped)
	- new(): an option given as undef now means "use the default"
	  instead of replacing the default with undef
	- process() with an undef sender records the message with an empty
	  Host column instead of dying inside Socket::getnameinfo
	- Windows: no longer dies because Fcntl does not define O_NOFOLLOW
	  there, or because Perl has no fchmod there (an existing log's
	  permissions are left alone); the tests skip what Windows cannot do (permission bits,
	  symlinks, signals) and the real-UDP test no longer relies on
	  alarm() interrupting recv()
	- run() no longer leaves the object marked as running when it dies
	  (e.g. a failed reopen after SIGHUP)
	- new(), open_socket(), reopen_log(), process() and run() no longer
	  change the caller's $! or $@
	- Log lines are written with syswrite: a failed write no longer
	  leaves data in Perl's buffer (which made the next close() die and
	  added an "unable to close filehandle" warning), and a line that
	  only partly fitted is removed, so the CSV never holds a half line
	- Loading the module at run time (require) no longer warns "Too late
	  to run CHECK block", and no longer leaves the private helpers
	  callable from outside
	- etc/syslogd flushes its status lines, so "listening" appears at
	  once under systemd or a pipe, not only at exit
	- Settings from Object::Configure (configuration files and
	  App__Syslogd__* environment variables) are validated like
	  arguments; they used to be accepted unchecked
	- Security: a FIFO at the log path is refused instead of hanging the
	  server for ever at start-up (O_NONBLOCK)
	- Security: control characters in reverse-DNS names are escaped in
	  the Host column, as they are in the message
	- A failing DNS cache (or one that answers nothing) falls back to the
	  address instead of killing process() and run()
	- A row that Text::CSV refuses is reported as a write failure instead
	  of being silently lost
	- parse_message() refuses references; a sender that is not a packed
	  address gives an empty Host instead of a Socket error
	- App::Syslogd::I18N::text() reports a missing key or values that are
	  not a hash reference clearly
	- Data-flow fixes: run() no longer leaves a SIGHUP request set for the
	  next run(); a failed or refused log open closes its handle at once;
	  a socket whose close() dies no longer leaves the log open; socket
	  errors no longer report a stale $IO::Socket::errstr; a write that
	  makes no progress reports "the system accepted no data" instead of
	  a stale $!
	- t/function.t: white-box tests of every function
	- t/unit.t: black-box tests of every documented message and state
	- t/edge_cases.t: hostile, boundary and security tests
	- t/data-flow.t: define-use chains and resource lifecycles
	- Logic reductions: run() calls open_socket() unconditionally (it is
	  a no-op when a socket is open); _receive() no longer re-checks the
	  socket, which run() guarantees, so the unreachable not_listening
	  message is gone; _csv_line() no longer re-checks string(), which
	  Text::CSV defines whenever combine() succeeds
	- App::Syslogd::I18N::text() reports its own errors with maketext()
	  instead of calling text() again, so a wrong guard can no longer
	  recurse for ever (found by mutation testing)
	- App::Syslogd::I18N::text() refuses "" and 0 as values, as its
	  domain says; they were silently treated as "no values"
	- Security: NUL bytes are refused in address, file and language; a
	  NUL made the server bind to a different address than it reported
	- POD: a SECURITY section (the attack surface and its protections)
	  and security invariants in the formal specification
	- t/cgi_security.t: simulated penetration tests (command injection,
	  traversal, NUL bytes, markup, line injection, taint mode)
	- t/path.t: one test for every path through every routine; path
	  analysis found no dead code and no loops to simplify
	- t/logic.t: truth tables of every boolean expression (with De Morgan
	  checks), the Z invariants before, during and after work, and every
	  transition of the state diagram
	- t/domain.t: equivalence partitions and boundaries of every input;
	  the POD now documents each input's domains
	- t/integration.t: end-to-end workflows, the program, and both
	  Text::CSV backends
	- Documentation rewritten in plain English, with ENCODING, COMMON
	  PITFALLS, FORMAL SPECIFICATION and STATE DIAGRAM sections
